  translation: "1. The blessed Lord said: Hear that by which you, with your mind fixed on me, practicing yoga and taking refuge in me alone, will know me without doubt, entire.\n2. I shall declare to you this knowledge together with direct experience, in full. Knowing it, nothing further that is to be known remains here.\n3. Among thousands of men, someone strives for success. Even of those who strive, and they are the successful, hardly one knows me as I truly am.\n4. Earth, water, fire, air, space, mind, and intellect too, and the thought \"I\": this, divided eightfold, is my nature.\n5. This is the lower nature. Other than it, know my higher nature, which has become the life-principle, O mighty-armed, the nature by which this world is held up.\n6. Understand that all beings have these two as their source. I am the origin of the whole world, and likewise its dissolution.\n7. There is nothing else higher than me, O Dhanaṃjaya. All this is strung on me, as a row of gems on a string.\n8. I am the taste in water, O son of Kuntī, and the light of the moon and the sun. I am the syllable Om in all the Vedas, sound in space, and manhood in men.\n9. I am the pure smell in the earth, and the brilliance in fire. I am the life in all beings, and the austerity in ascetics.\n10. Know me, O Pārtha, as the ancient seed of all beings. I am the intellect of those who have it, and the boldness of those who have that.\n11. And I am the strength of the strong, the strength free of desire and passion. I am the desire in beings that scripture does not forbid, O best of the Bharatas.\n12. Whatever states arise from sattva, from rajas, or from tamas, know them as come from me alone. Even so I am not in them. They are in me.\n13. Deluded by these three states made of the qualities, this whole world does not know me, who am beyond them and imperishable.\n14. This divine power of appearance, mine and made of the qualities, is hard to cross. Those who take refuge in me alone cross this power.\n15. Evildoers, the deluded, the lowest of men, do not take refuge in me. Their knowledge stolen by the power of appearance, they have taken to a demonic nature.\n16. Four kinds of people of good deeds worship me, O Arjuna: the one in distress, the one who wishes to know, the one who seeks wealth, and the knower, O best of the Bharatas.\n17. Of those four, the knower stands apart: he is always steadfast, and his devotion is to one alone. For I am the Self of the knower, and so exceedingly dear, and he is my very Self, and so dear to me.\n18. All these are noble, but the knower is my very self: that is my settled view. For he, his mind steadied, has set out toward me alone, the highest goal.\n19. At the end of many births, one whose knowledge has ripened comes to me. He is the great self, very hard to find: he knows that Vāsudeva is everything.\n20. Those whose knowledge has been seized by one desire and another resort to other deities. They take up this rule and that, held to it by their own nature.\n21. Whoever, as a devotee, wishes with faith to worship whichever form of a deity, I make that very faith of his unwavering.\n22. Endowed with that faith, he pursues the worship of that deity, and from her he obtains those desires, for they are ordained by me alone.\n23. But that fruit of theirs comes to an end, for they are of small wit. Those who worship the gods go to the gods; my devotees come to me as well.\n24. The undiscerning think that I, the unmanifest, have taken on manifestation. They do not know my supreme state, imperishable and unsurpassed.\n25. I am not manifest to everyone, wrapped as I am in the arranging of the guṇas, which is itself māyā. This deluded world does not know me as unborn and imperishable.\n26. I know the beings that have passed, O Arjuna, and those that are present, and those that will be. But no one knows me, except the one devotee who has taken refuge.\n27. By the delusion caused by the pairs, risen from desire and aversion, all beings fall into delusion at their very birth, O Bhārata, O scorcher of the foe.\n28. But those people of good deeds whose evil has nearly run out, freed from the delusion of the pairs, worship me, firm in their vow.\n29. Those who strive, taking refuge in me, for release from old age and death — they know that Brahman, and the whole of what concerns the self, and action in full.\n30. Those who know me as present with what pertains to creatures and to the gods, and with what pertains to sacrifice — they, their minds steadied, know me even at the time of death.",
